{
  "title": "MIG GPT: Harnessing Large Language Models for Automated Migration of Out-of-Tree Linux Kernel Patches",
  "authors": [
    "Pucheng Dang",
    "Di Huang",
    "Dong Li",
    "Kang Chen",
    "Yuanbo Wen",
    "Qi Guo",
    "Xing Hu"
  ],
  "year": 2025,
  "arxivId": "2504.09474",
  "abstract": "MIG GPT is a framework that assists humans in automating the migration and maintenance of out-of-tree kernel patches across Linux kernel versions. It leverages large language models (LLMs) and a novel code fingerprint structure to improve migration accuracy and efficiency. MIG GPT outperforms vanilla LLMs, achieving an average completion rate of 74.07% for migration tasks, and requires only 2.08% of the average time taken by human experts.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Core Idea",
      "content": "The Linux kernel is widely used and has many out-of-tree patches that need to be maintained and updated. <strong>Large Language Models (LLMs)</strong> have shown great promise in understanding and generating code, but they struggle with incomplete code context understanding and inaccurate migration point identification. MIG GPT addresses these challenges by employing a novel <strong>code fingerprint structure</strong> to retain code snippet information and incorporating three core modules to improve migration accuracy and efficiency.",
